  "agent": {
    "note": "**W1 — canvas tiles collapsed to a CONSTANT source set (perf lock). Rebuild templates + reload.**\n\nThe canvas was **93.8% of the 10×10 render** (228.2s of 243.2s). Root cause: **one text source per tile** (100 at 10×10), each carrying a per-frame `alpha: fadeInExpr` — which forces a **per-pixel `geq` alpha fold** — plus ~100 PNG inputs feeding swscale.\n\nNow the whole grid is a **fixed set of full-canvas sources**, independent of tile count:\n\n| source | what |\n|---|---|\n| 1 inline-mask atlas | all tile **fills** — rounded corners kept (SVG rounding path, not geq) |\n| 1 inline-mask atlas | hairline **borders** (the old inner stroke) |\n| 1 static text atlas | all **numbers + dims** baked into ONE still PNG |\n| 1 drawbox **curtain** | the **reveal** — enable-only, zero geq (the cursor/split idiom) |\n\n**The trade-off you asked to see:** the per-tile **fade-in is gone** — tiles now **hard-cut** in at their reveal moment (the same hard snap the orange cursor + dashed splits already use). There is now **zero `geq` anywhere** in the canvas.\n\nSmoke (640×360, 3×3): canvas self-time **0.3s / ~4%** — from the dominant node to noise. Full 10×10 numbers are your re-render (one-liner in the handoff), compared against `perf_10x10`.\n\n*(Still open from 034: whether default 1920×1080 fills the panel cell vs. keeps true-aspect bars — say the word if you want that too.)*",
    "question": "Is the ~10× canvas perf win worth losing the per-tile fade-in (reveal is now a hard cut, like the cursor/splits)? And do the rounded corners / hairline borders / numbers still read right at 10×10?",
    "response": {
      "body": "perf win confirmed. per tile fade in is not missed that much.",
      "from": "human",
      "at": "2026-07-02T18:25:50.909Z",
      "src": "<sandbox>/sessions/2026-06-28-dsl-tutorial/candidate-035-4-1920x1080.mp4"
    }
  }
